// The virtual card's save, in Ruby and in the port (register row 33,
// card-mod-virtual).
//
// RUBY (card-mod-virtual-0.20.0 set/abstract/virtual_cache.rb:21-29):
//
//   event :save_virtual_content, :prepare_to_store, on: :save do
//     Virtual.save self
//     abort :success
//   end
//
//   event :delete_virtual_content, :prepare_to_store, on: :delete do
//     Virtual.delete self
//     abort :success unless real?
//   end
//
// The abort unwinds the save, so a virtual card's OWN cards row is never
// written: the content lives in the card_virtuals row the Virtual.save
// wrote, and nothing else.
//
// THE THREE MECHANISMS, CLOSED (the row's measured gap and its repair):
//
// 1. THE PORT'S ABORT IS A THROW, AND THE BODY THROWS IT. The port models
//    abort :success as the ModEventAbortSuccess throw
//    (src/card/actions/types.ts). The registered bodies are the Ruby
//    bodies: they run Virtual.save / Virtual.delete and throw the abort.
//
// 2. THE WRITE ORDER RUNS THE BODIES, AND THE ROW WRITE NEVER COMES. Both
//    events are write-order owned (card-action-engine.ts
//    WRITE_ORDER_OWNED_EVENTS): the create arm (after the ordinary
//    validation phase), the update arm (before the row write) and the
//    delete arm (before the trash write) run the registered body with the
//    virtualContent seat and answer its abort with the no-write success,
//    returning rather than throwing so the card_virtuals write persists.
//    The Virtual side effect runs exactly once and the cards row is never
//    written. Without a registered body the arms run the mod's exported
//    bodies directly.
//
// 3. THE SET CHAIN RESOLVES. Ruby's only include_set call site in the
//    pinned tree is card-mod-assets set/right/asset_input.rb:1 `include_set
//    Abstract::VirtualCache`, so the set is exactly the RIGHT asset_input
//    cards. The generated view graph (set-include-graph.ts) records view
//    modules and names no includer; the event dispatch's membership rides
//    the serving runtime's setMember instead, whose abstract/virtual_cache
//    arm (src/server/write-routes.ts, the abstract/attachment arm's
//    shape) resolves the same membership.

